  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**Investigation focus:**\u202fA disruption at **Chevron** reported around\u202f2012\u201108\u201106.  \nThe goal was to identify downstream (or alternative\u2011supplier) firms that could be structurally at risk from this event and to back each candidate with evidence from the SEC filing tools.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Structural\u2010risk candidates (downstream of Chevron)\n\nThe **traverse_supply_graph** call (mode\u202f=\u202f\u201cdownstream\u201d, max_tier\u202f=\u202f2) returned a ranked list of 82 enriched companies. The highest\u2011confidence Tier\u20111 firms \u2013 those most directly linked to Chevron\u2019s output \u2013 were:\n\n| Tier | Company (as listed) | Industry / Role |\n|------|---------------------|-----------------|\n| 1 | **Toyota Motor** | Automotive OEM \u2013 uses refined fuels, lubricants and petrochemical feedstocks from Chevron. |\n| 1 | **Cummins** | Diesel\u2011engine & alternative\u2011fuel engine maker \u2013 sources diesel fuel and related oil products. |\n| 1 | **Indian Oil** | Large Indian refin\u00ader \u2013 purchases crude and refined products. |\n| 1 | **Jordan Petroleum Refinery** | Regional refinery \u2013 consumes crude and produces fuels that feed downstream users. |\n| 1 | **SK (South Korea)** | Holding with energy\u2011related subsidiaries; sources crude & refined products. |\n| 1 | **CIA Siderurgica Nacional** | Steel producer \u2013 uses fuel and coke derived from Chevron\u2019s petroleum stream. |\n| 1 | **e1 (Korea)** | LPG importer/distributor \u2013 directly dependent on Chevron\u2019s LPG output. |\n| 1 | **Lanxess** | Specialty\u2011chemical maker \u2013 buys petrochemical intermediates. |\n\nTier\u202f2 added many automotive OEMs, component suppliers and other oil producers (e.g., Mazda, General Motors, Exxon\u202fMobil, BP, etc.). Because the investigation budget is limited to four turns, I focused on the highest\u2011confidence Tier\u20111 firms that are most likely to feel a material impact.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Evidence gathered\n\n| Company | Tool(s) used | What was found |\n|---------|--------------|----------------|\n| **Toyota Motor** | `get_supplier_info` (20\u2011F filing, 2012\u201106\u201125) \u2013\u202fthe only filing that falls within the pre\u2011event window. The document contains the standard \u201cRisk Factors\u201d and \u201cBusiness Overview\u201d sections but **does not mention Chevron or any disruption affecting its fuel supply**. No explicit reference to a Chevron event was located in the 20\u2011F excerpt. | *No direct evidence of impact.* |\n| **Toyota Motor** (second source) | `search_corpus` filtered for \u201cToyota Motor\u201d & query\u202f=\u202f\u201cChevron\u201d, date\u202f=\u202f2012\u201108\u201106. The top five matches were all unrelated regulatory or emissions discussions; none referenced Chevron\u2019s disruption. | *No corroborating mention of Chevron.* |\n| **Cummins** | `get_supplier_info` \u2013 attempted for \u201cCummins Inc\u201d. The system returned **\u201cNo filings on record\u201d**, meaning no SEC filing was available in the corpus for Cummins at that time (or under that exact name). | *Unable to confirm any impact; no filing to examine.* |\n| **Indian Oil** | No tool calls were made because of turn limits. It remains a high\u2011confidence downstream user, but I could not retrieve evidence. |\n| **Jordan Petroleum Refinery**, **SK**, **CIA Siderurgica Nacional**, **e1**, **Lanxess** \u2013 likewise, no additional filings or corpus searches were performed due to the turn budget; therefore their exposure is unverified.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What could NOT be confirmed\n\n| Company | Reason for missing evidence |\n|---------|-----------------------------|\n| **Toyota Motor** \u2013 despite being a top\u2011tier downstream customer, neither its 20\u2011F filing nor the corpus search referenced Chevron\u2019s August\u202f2012 disruption. |\n| **Cummins Inc** \u2013 no SEC filing exists in the dataset; therefore I could not assess any mention of Chevron. |\n| **Indian Oil**, **Jordan Petroleum Refinery**, **SK**, **CIA Siderurgica Nacional**, **e1**, **Lanxess** \u2013 all appear as high\u2011confidence downstream entities, but I was unable to run additional `get_supplier_info` or targeted `search_corpus` calls for them because the investigation reached its four\u2011turn limit. |\n| All Tier\u202f2 firms (Mazda, General Motors, Exxon\u202fMobil, BP, etc.) \u2013 similarly unexamined due to budget constraints.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Overall assessment\n\n- **Structural risk:** The supply\u2011graph shows a broad network of downstream users that could be affected by a Chevron disruption, especially large fuel consumers and automotive OEMs.\n- **Evidence status:** For the only company I was able to pull a filing for (Toyota Motor), there is **no explicit mention** of any Chevron\u2011related supply issue around the event date. Cummins could not be examined because no filing exists in the corpus. All other high\u2011confidence downstream firms remain **unverified** due to tool\u2011call limits.\n- **Conclusion:** While the graph suggests many companies are *potentially* at risk, **the available documentary evidence does not confirm that any of the identified Tier\u20111 downstream firms publicly disclosed an impact from Chevron\u2019s August\u202f2012 disruption**. Further investigation (additional turns or external data sources) would be required to validate exposure for Indian Oil, Jordan Petroleum Refinery, SK, CIA Siderurgica Nacional, e1, Lanxess, and the numerous automotive OEMs in Tier\u202f2.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (81) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - e1: no evidence gathered\n  - lanxess: no evidence gathered\n  - sk: no evidence gathered\n  - cia siderurgica nacional: no evidence gathered\n  - jordan petroleum refinery: no evidence gathered\n  - cummins: no evidence gathered\n  - indian oil: no evidence gathered